[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Test-Driven Development (TDD) and SOLID as a Testing Enabler"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Writing maintainable software requires more than just making code work; it requires designing systems that are resilient to change. By combining Test-Driven Development (TDD) with the SOLID principles, developers can create architectures that are naturally testable, decoupled, and robust."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Synergy Between TDD and SOLID"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"TDD forces you to write tests before implementation. When you try to test tightly coupled, monolithic code first, you immediately hit friction: complex mocking setups, hidden dependencies, and brittle assertions. This friction is a direct symptom of violating SOLID principles. Thus, TDD acts as an early warning system for poor design."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Single Responsibility Principle (SRP): TDD encourages small, focused units of behavior, making it easier to write isolated unit tests."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP): TDD relies heavily on interfaces and mocks, which naturally leads to injecting dependencies rather than instantiating them locally."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Refactoring via DIP: A Practical Example"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a tightly coupled class that directly instantiates a database connection. Writing a unit test for this requires hitting a real database, making the test slow and unreliable."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"class UserService {
    private SqlDatabase database = new SqlDatabase();
    public void registerUser(String email) {
        database.save(email);
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"By applying the Dependency Inversion Principle, we introduce an interface. This allows us to pass a mock database during our TDD cycle."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"interface Database {
    void save(String data);
}

class UserService {
    private final Database database;

    public UserService(Database database) {
        this.database = database;
    }

    public void registerUser(String email) {
        database.save(email);
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"TDD and SOLID form a symbiotic relationship. TDD exposes architectural flaws through testing pain, while SOLID provides the design patterns needed to resolve that pain. Embracing both ensures a highly testable, scalable, and maintainable codebase."}]
